Shared counter using std::mutex is a classic C++ multithreading interview problem. The key idea is: protect every access to the shared counter with the same mutex.

#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>
#include <vector>

class Counter {
private:
    int count_ = 0;
    std::mutex mutex_;

public:
    void increment() {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        ++count_;
    }

    int get() {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        return count_;
    }
};

void worker(Counter& counter, int iterations) {
    for (int i = 0; i < iterations; ++i) {
        counter.increment();
    }
}

int main() {
    Counter counter;

    constexpr int numThreads = 10;
    constexpr int iterations = 100000;

    std::vector<std::thread> threads;

    for (int i = 0; i < numThreads; ++i) {
        threads.emplace_back(worker,
                             std::ref(counter),
                             iterations);
    }

    for (auto& t : threads) {
        t.join();
    }

    std::cout << "Final count = " << counter.get() << '\n';

    return 0;
}

Expected output-

Final count = 1000000

Because:

10 threads × 100,000 increments = 1,000,000

Why do we need the mutex?
This:

++count_;

looks like one operation, but conceptually it involves:
1. Read count_
2. Add 1
3. Write count_

Without synchronization, two threads can do:
Thread 1                  Thread 2
--------                  --------
read count = 10
                          read count = 10
write 11
                          write 11

The expected result was 12, but we get 11. This is a data race and therefore undefined behavior in C++.

With:

std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
only one thread can execute the critical section at a time.

Why lock_guard?
This is an excellent staff-level interview point.
std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
uses RAII:
constructor → locks mutex
scope       → critical section
destructor  → unlocks mutex

So even if an exception occurs, the mutex is automatically released.
You could technically write:
mutex_.lock();
++count_;
mutex_.unlock();

but this is dangerous because an early return or exception can leave the mutex locked.

Important interview consideration
If get() is called concurrently with increment(), it must also be synchronized:

int get() {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
    return count_;
}

A common mistake is to protect writes but not reads:

void increment() {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
    ++count_;
}

int get() {
    return count_;       // ❌ data race if increment() is running
}

For a staff-level interview, you should explicitly mention:
Every access to shared mutable state must obey the same synchronization protocol.

Alternative: direct shared counter
For a simpler interview answer:

#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>
#include <vector>

int counter = 0;
std::mutex m;

void incrementCounter(int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(m);
        ++counter;
    }
}

int main() {
    std::vector<std::thread> threads;

    for (int i = 0; i < 10; ++i) {
        threads.emplace_back(incrementCounter, 100000);
    }

    for (auto& t : threads) {
        t.join();
    }

    std::cout << counter << '\n';
}

The class-based version is preferable in a staff-level interview because it encapsulates the shared state and its synchronization, preventing 
callers from accidentally accessing count_ without the mutex.
